 NOTES = r"""
-Definition. Section 1 defines a (d,k)-CSP assignment as a point of [d]^n.
-Section 3 replaces Hamming distance by shortest-path distance in a graph G on
-the colors and lifts it additively to assignments. For the directed cycle C_d,
-the one-coordinate distances from any center are exactly 0,1,...,d-1. Lemma
-3.2 therefore identifies T(m,r), the number of assignments at exact distance
-r, with [x^r](1+x+...+x^(d-1))^m. This is the native object used here; no graph,
-SAT, finite-field, or integer-coordinate surrogate replaces it.
+Definition and native objects. Section 1 of Scheder's paper defines variables
+colored from [d], literals (x_i != c), constraints as ORs of literals, and a CSP
+formula as their conjunction. This module uses d=3 and constraints of arity at
+most three verbatim. In the renderer, a forbidden tuple abc on scope [i,j,k]
+is only shorthand for the paper's clause
+  (x_i != a) OR (x_j != b) OR (x_k != c).
+The witness is the paper's own satisfying assignment, and verify() checks every
+such clause directly. No finite-field instance is substituted for the CSP.
 
-Step-0 decision. Theorem 3.3 is an upper-bound algorithm, not evidence that a
-random planted CSP distribution is hard. The prior planted-assignment triage
-was therefore not used. The paragraph immediately after Theorem 3.3 says that
-the maximizing coefficients T(m,r) can be computed quickly by dynamic
-programming. That rules out an honest Track-A claim but supplies the Track-B
-mechanical route. The implemented sliding-window recurrence performs O(mr)
-exact additions/subtractions. At the named hard preset this is roughly two
-million operations. In contrast, the identity
-  (1+x+...+x^(d-1))^m = (1-x^d)^m (1-x)^(-m)
-gives the r-th coefficient as a sum over j=0..floor(r/d) of
-  (-1)^j C(m,j) C(r-j*d+m-1,m-1).
-The hard preset fixes floor(r/d)=11, so writing the certificate takes twelve
-terms and at most 72 symbolic/index operations. The certificate is produced by
-composition of these two formal-power-series identities, never by running the
-dynamic program on the generated instance.
+Step-0 and track decision. The paper's main theorem gives a deterministic
+exponential algorithm for general (d,k)-CSP; at d=k=3 its displayed bound is
+about 2.077^n. That theorem is not an average-case lower bound for planted
+formulas, so this module makes no Track-A claim. Its promised distribution has
+an efficient specialized algorithm: recover the ternary-linear relation behind
+each complete forbidden-tuple block and run exact Gaussian elimination. Native
+DPLL with unit propagation is also measured and reported as the domain-standard
+reference_algorithm, as Track B requires.
 
-Generation and verification. The center assignment is sampled uniformly and
-the alphabet size and radius are sampled before the symbolic identity is
-instantiated. Center relabellings and coordinate permutations are genuine
-isometries, and the exact sphere count is independent of the center. verify()
-never reads inst["answer"]: it validates the bounded expression grammar, uses
-two modular filters, evaluates a surviving expression over the integers, and
-compares it with an independently cached sliding-window dynamic program.
+Generation route. Variables carry distinct nonzero vectors of a base-3 vector
+space. The generator samples a quadratic monomial Q(v)=v_p*v_q, an affine
+linear form, and a constant, then samples the satisfying colors first. Every
+block is subsequently made from a two- or three-variable linear equation whose
+right side is evaluated on that planted assignment. A triangular set of core
+relations determines all non-basis variables from the basis variables, proving
+that exactly 3^q assignments satisfy the formula. Extra relations are sampled
+from the same vector-addition identity and are redundant. The certificate is
+therefore known by inverse generation, never by solving the produced formula.
 
-Easy regimes and attacks. Very small r or r<d makes the unbounded
-stars-and-bars term correct and is deliberately avoided outside the demo.
-Every non-demo radius crosses several upper-boundary layers. The outlier attack
-keeps only the largest boundary term; greedy ignores the upper color bound;
-random restart samples the full declared certificate language; and the
-in-context truncation attack keeps only the first two inclusion-exclusion
-terms. The successful dynamic program is reported separately, as Track B
-requires. Difficulty escalation enlarges d and r while keeping the twelve-term
-witness fixed.
+Compact route. For f(v)=v_p*v_q+L(v)+c over GF(3), the affine part cancels from
+f(u)+f(v)-f(u+v). Binary basis-doubling relations reveal c, while the basis-pair
+relations have one exceptional pair revealing p,q. Choosing L=0 then gives the
+valid assignment f(v)=v_p*v_q+c. At n=80 this is at most 240 per-coordinate
+multiply/add/reduce operations plus ten comparisons: 250 total. The full
+reference route recognizes hundreds of clause blocks and eliminates a dense
+matrix instead.
 
-Canonicalization. Permuting coordinates, independently translating each
-center color around C_d, and composing those maps preserve the problem and its
-answer. The canonical key removes the center entirely and retains (m,d,r), the
-complete invariant of the sphere-count question. It contains neither seed nor
-rendered text.
+Easy regimes and attacks. A numeric/topological variable order made greedy
+propagation recover the answer, so construction now randomly permutes variables
+after all relations are built. Complete linear blocks are color-balanced, so
+per-variable forbidden-tuple frequency carries no planted-color signal. The
+panel also tests sequential greedy assignment, uniform random restarts, and all
+affine functions of the displayed labels; the last is the natural in-context
+ansatz and fails because the cross term is genuinely quadratic.
+
+Canonicalization. Reordering blocks, renaming variables while carrying their
+labels, and independently translating each variable's three colors are genuine
+CSP isomorphisms. The cheap key uses block arities, variable incidence profiles,
+and pair co-incidence multiplicities, ignoring right sides because all generated
+consistent right sides on a fixed row system differ by a color translation.
+Full hypergraph isomorphism is not attempted; this limitation is stated in the
+README.
