A substantial draft already exists in the workspace, so I’m auditing rather than replacing it blindly. The current construction passes its internal gates, but its own DPLL baseline solves in only five nodes and the claimed 250-operation shortcut omits the cost of recognizing hundreds of shuffled clause blocks. That is not yet an honest Track B compression gap. I’m testing a repair that keeps the paper’s native clauses but replaces the propagation-friendly triangular bulk with planted random ternary relations; the small polarization fingerprint remains the compact route.
